/**
  @page guides-solvers Solvers
  @brief Guide to linear and nonlinear solvers in Rodin

  @tableofcontents

  @m_footernavigation

  @section guides-solvers-intro Introduction

  After assembling a finite element problem, the resulting linear system

  @f[
    A \mathbf{x} = \mathbf{b}
  @f]

  must be solved. Rodin provides several solvers in the @ref Rodin::Solver
  namespace, ranging from direct methods to iterative solvers.

  @section guides-solvers-available Available Solvers

  | Solver | Type | Best For | Symmetric Required |
  |--------|------|----------|-------------------|
  | @ref Rodin::Solver::SparseLU "SparseLU" | Direct | Small to medium problems | No |
  | @ref Rodin::Solver::CG "CG" | Iterative | Large SPD systems | Yes |
  | @ref Rodin::Solver::UMFPack "UMFPack" | Direct | General sparse systems | No |

  @section guides-solvers-direct Direct Solvers

  Direct solvers compute the exact solution (up to floating-point precision)
  by factoring the system matrix. They are reliable but can be expensive
  for very large systems.

  @subsection guides-solvers-direct-sparselu SparseLU

  A sparse LU decomposition solver. Works for any non-singular matrix:

  @code{.cpp}
  #include <Rodin/Solver.h>

  using namespace Rodin::Solver;

  // Solve the problem
  SparseLU().solve(problem);
  @endcode

  SparseLU is a good default choice when:
  - The system is not too large (up to ~100,000 DOFs)
  - You need a robust solver that works for any matrix
  - The matrix is non-symmetric

  @section guides-solvers-iterative Iterative Solvers

  Iterative solvers approximate the solution through successive iterations.
  They are more efficient for large systems but may require tuning.

  @subsection guides-solvers-iterative-cg Conjugate Gradient (CG)

  The Conjugate Gradient method is optimal for **symmetric positive definite**
  (SPD) systems, which arise from many common PDEs (Poisson, elasticity, heat):

  @code{.cpp}
  // Basic usage
  CG(problem).solve();

  // With options
  CG solver(problem);
  solver.setMaxIterations(1000);
  solver.setRelativeTolerance(1e-10);
  solver.solve();
  @endcode

  CG is the recommended solver when:
  - The system matrix is symmetric positive definite
  - The system is large (100,000+ DOFs)
  - You want efficient iterative solving

  @section guides-solvers-usage Usage Patterns

  @subsection guides-solvers-usage-inline Inline Solving

  For quick usage, create and invoke the solver inline:

  @code{.cpp}
  Solver::CG(poisson).solve();
  @endcode

  @subsection guides-solvers-usage-configured Configured Solving

  For more control, create the solver separately:

  @code{.cpp}
  Solver::CG solver(poisson);
  solver.setMaxIterations(500);
  solver.setRelativeTolerance(1e-12);
  solver.solve();
  @endcode

  @section guides-solvers-choosing Choosing a Solver

  | Criterion | Recommended Solver |
  |-----------|-------------------|
  | Small system, any matrix | SparseLU |
  | Large symmetric system | CG |
  | Non-symmetric system | SparseLU or UMFPack |
  | Need guaranteed convergence | Direct solver |
  | Memory-constrained | CG |

  @section guides-solvers-example Complete Example

  @code{.cpp}
  #include <Rodin/Solver.h>
  #include <Rodin/Geometry.h>
  #include <Rodin/Variational.h>
  #include <Rodin/IO/XDMF.h>

  using namespace Rodin;
  using namespace Rodin::Geometry;
  using namespace Rodin::Variational;

  int main()
  {
    Mesh mesh;
    mesh = mesh.UniformGrid(Polytope::Type::Triangle, {32, 32});
    mesh.getConnectivity().compute(1, 2);

    P1 Vh(mesh);
    TrialFunction u(Vh);
    TestFunction  v(Vh);

    Problem poisson(u, v);
    poisson = Integral(Grad(u), Grad(v))
            - Integral(RealFunction(1.0), v)
            + DirichletBC(u, Zero());

    // Solve with CG
    Solver::CG(poisson).solve();

    // Export result
    IO::XDMF xdmf("SolverExample");
    xdmf.grid().setMesh(mesh).add("u", u.getSolution());
    xdmf.write();

    return 0;
  }
  @endcode

  @section guides-solvers-see-also See Also

  - @ref Rodin::Solver "Solver namespace reference"
  - @ref guides-weak-formulations "Weak formulations"
  - @ref examples-pdes-poisson "Poisson example"

  */
